\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Chapter 12: M-Estimation and Nonlinear Regression
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
12.1 Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We begin our study of nonlinear estimation with a general class of estimators
 known as M-estimators.
 M-estimaton methods include maximum likelihood, nonlinear least squares,
 least absolute deviations, quasi-maximum likelihood, and many other procedures.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In a 
\series bold
nonlinear regression model
\series default
, we have a random variable, 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, and we would like to model 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y|x)$
\end_inset

 as a function of the explanatory variables 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, a 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

-vector.
 We already know how to estimate models of 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y|x)$
\end_inset

 when the model model is linear in parameters.
 What happens if the regression function is nonlinear in its parameters?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Generally, let 
\begin_inset Formula $m(x,\theta)$
\end_inset

 be a 
\series bold
parametric model
\series default
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y|x)$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 is a 
\emph on
known
\emph default
 function of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $P\times1$
\end_inset

 parameter vector.
 (This is a parametric model because 
\begin_inset Formula $m(\cdot,\theta)$
\end_inset

 is assumed to be known up to a 
\emph on
finite
\emph default
 number of parameters.) The dimension of the parameters, 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

, can be less than or greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
parameter space
\series default
, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

, is a subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{P}$
\end_inset

.
 This is the set of values of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 that we are willing to consider in the regression function.
 Unlike in linear models, for nonlinear models the asymptotic analysis requires
 explicit assumptions on the parameter space.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In any application, there is no guarantee that our chosen model is adequate
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y|x)$
\end_inset

.
 We say that we have a 
\series bold
correctly specified model for the conditional mean
\series default
, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y|x)$
\end_inset

, if, for some 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}\in\Theta$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(y|x)=m(x,\theta_{o})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We introduce the subscript 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

o
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 on theta to distinguish the parameter vector appearing in 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y|x)$
\end_inset

 from other candidates for that vector.
 Often, the value 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 is called 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

the true value of theta
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

the true parameter.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 We will never know the actual 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

, but, if the model is correctly specified, then the true value exists,
 and we would like to estimate it.
 In our theoretical development, we need to distinguish the vector appearing
 in 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y|x)$
\end_inset

 from a generic element of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Equation (1) is the most general way of thinking about what nonlinear least
 squares is inteded to do: estimate models of conditional expectations.
 But, as a statistical matter, equation (1) is equivalent to a model with
 an additive, unobservalbe error with a zero conditional mean:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y=m(x,\theta_{o})+u,\ E(u|x)=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Given equation (2), equation (1) clearly holds.
 Conversely, given equation (1), we obtain equation (2) by 
\emph on
defining
\emph default
 the error to be 
\begin_inset Formula $u\equiv y-m(x,\theta_{o})$
\end_inset

.
 We must remember that, in writing the model in additive error form, the
 
\emph on
only
\emph default
 thing implied by equation (1) is 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u|x)=0$
\end_inset

.
 Depending on the nature of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, the error 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 may have some unusual properties.
 For example, if 
\begin_inset Formula $y\geq0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $u\geq-m(x,\theta_{o})$
\end_inset

, in which case 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 cannot be independent.
 Heteroskedasticity is present whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(y|x)$
\end_inset

 depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, as is very common when 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 takes on a restricted range of values.
 So, for a random sample of observations, the assumption that 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 are i.i.d.
 is often much too strong.
 However, the error form of the model does turn out to be usefull for defining
 estimators of asymptotic variances and for obtaining test statistics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We formalize the first 
\series bold
nonlinear least squares (NLS)
\series default
 assumption as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\shape up
NLS.1:
\series default
 
\emph on
For some 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}\in\Theta$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y|x)=m(x,\theta_{o})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In our general development of M-estimators that follows, we will need to
 add conditions involving moments of 
\begin_inset Formula $m(x,\theta)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, as well as continuity assumptions on 
\begin_inset Formula $m(x,\cdot)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If we let 
\begin_inset Formula $w\equiv(x,y)$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 indexes a feature of the population distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

, namely, the conditional mean of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 More generally, let 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 be an 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

-vector of random variables with some distribution in the population.
 We let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{W}$
\end_inset

 denote the subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{M}$
\end_inset

 representing the possible values of 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 denote a parameter vector describing some feature of the distribution of
 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

.
 This could be a conditional mean, a conditional mean and conditional variance,
 a conditional median, or a conditional distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We assume that our data come as a random sample of size 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 from the population; we label this random sample 
\begin_inset Formula $\{w_{i}:i=1,,\ldots,N\}$
\end_inset

, where each 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 is an 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

-vector.
 This assumption covers cross section models with many equations, and it
 also covers panel data settings with small time series dimension.
 In the NLS, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 consists of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

, the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

th draw from the population on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and y.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What allows use to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 when it indexes 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y|x)$
\end_inset

? It is the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 is the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 that minimizes the expected squared error between 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $m(x,\theta)$
\end_inset

.
 This is 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 solves the population problem
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \min_{\theta\in\Theta}}\ E\left\{ \left[y-m(x,\theta)\right]^{2}\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the expectation is over the joint distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)$
\end_inset

.
 This conclustion follows immediately from basic properties of conditional
 expectations.
 Write
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\left[y-m(x,\theta)\right]^{2} & = & \left[(y-m(x,\theta_{o})+(m(x,\theta_{o})-m(x,\theta))\right]^{2}\nonumber \\
 & = & \left[y-m(x,\theta_{o})\right]^{2}+2\left[m(x,\theta_{o})-m(x,\theta)\right]u+\left[m(x,\theta_{o})-m(x,\theta)\right]^{2}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is defined in equation (2).
 Now, since 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u|x)=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is uncorrelated with 
\emph on
any
\emph default
 function of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, including 
\begin_inset Formula $m(x,\theta_{o})-m(x,\theta)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, taking the expected value of equation (4) gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E\left\{ \left[y-m(x,\theta)\right]^{2}\right\} =E\left\{ \left[y-m(x,\theta_{o})\right]^{2}\right\} +E\left\{ \left[m(x,\theta_{o})-m(x,\theta)\right]^{2}\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Since the last term in equation (5) is nonnegative, it follws that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E\left\{ \left[y-m(x,\theta)\right]^{2}\right\} \geq E\left\{ \left[y-m(x,\theta_{o})\right]^{2}\right\} ,\ \forall\ \theta\in\Theta
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The inequality is strict when 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\neq\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 unless 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left\{ \left[m(x,\theta_{o})-m(x,\theta)\right]^{2}\right\} =0$
\end_inset

; for 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 to be identified, we will have to rule this possibility out.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Because 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 solves the population problem in expression (3), the analogy principle
 suggests etimating 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 by solving the sample analogue.
 In other words, we replace the population moment 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left\{ \left[y-m(x,\theta)\right]^{2}\right\} $
\end_inset

 with the sample average.
 The 
\series bold
NLS estimator
\series default
\emph on
 
\emph default
of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

, solves
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \min_{\theta\in\Theta}}\ N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\left[y_{i}-m(x_{i},\theta)\right]^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

For now, we assume that a solution to this problem exists.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The NLS objective funciton in expression (7) is a special case of a more
 general class of estimators.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $q(w,\theta)$
\end_inset

 be a function of the random vector 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 and the parameter vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
 An 
\series bold
M-estimator
\series default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 solves the problem
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \min_{\theta\in\Theta}}\ N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}q(w_{i},\theta)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

assuming that a solution, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

, exists.
 The estimator clearly depends on the sample 
\begin_inset Formula $\{w_{i}:i=1,,\ldots,N\}$
\end_inset

, but we suppress that fact in the notation.
 The objective funciton for an M-estimator is a sample average of a function
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The parameter vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 is assumed to uniquely solve the population problem
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \min_{\theta\in\Theta}}\ E\left[q(w,\theta)\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Comparing equations (8) and (9), we see that M-estimators are based on the
 analogy principle.
 Once 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 has been defined, finding an appropriate function 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 that delivers 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 as the solution to problem (9) requires basic results from probability
 theory.
 Often there is more than one choice of 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 solves problem (9), in which case the choice depends on efficiency or computati
onal issues.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

How do we translate the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 solves the population problem (9) into consistency of the M-estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 that solves problem (8)? Since for each 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\in\Theta$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ q(w_{i},\theta):i=1,\ldots,N\right\} $
\end_inset

 is just an i.i.d.
 sequence, the law of large numbers implies that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}q(w_{i},\theta)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}E\left[q(w,\theta)\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

under very weak finite moment assumptions.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 minimizes the function on the left side of equation (1) and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 minimizes the function on the right, it seems plausible that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

.
 This informal argument turns out to be correct, except in pathological
 cases.
 We also need to address the identifiability of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 and the convergence in equation (10).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
12.2 Identification, Uniform Convergence, and Consistency
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now present a formal consistency result for M-estimators under fairly
 weak assumptions.
 As mentioned previously, the conditions can be broken down into two parts.
 The first part is the 
\series bold
identification
\series default
 or 
\series bold
identifiability
\series default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 .
 For nonlinear regression, we showed how 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 solves the population problem (3).
 However, we did not argue that 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 is always the 
\emph on
unique
\emph default
 solution to problem (3).
 Whether or not this is the case depends on the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and the nature of the regression function:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\shape up
NLS.2:
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left\{ \left[m(x,\theta_{o})-m(x,\theta)\right]^{2}\right\} >0,\ \forall\ \theta\in\Theta,\theta\neq\theta_{o}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This assumption can fail if the explanatory variables 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 do not have sufficient variation in the population.
 In nonlinear models, Assumption NLS.2 can fail if 
\begin_inset Formula $m(x,\theta_{o})$
\end_inset

 depends on fewer parameters than are actually in 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Identification in commonly used nonlinear regression models holds under
 weak conditions, provided perfect collinearity in 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 can be ruled out.
 For the most part, we will just assume that, when the model is correctly
 specified, 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 is the unique solution to problem (3).
 For the general M-estimation case, we assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $q(w,\theta)$
\end_inset

 has been chosen so that 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 is a solution to problem (9).
 Identification requires that 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 be the unique solution
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E\left[q(w,\theta_{o})\right]<E\left[q(w,\theta)\right],\ \forall\ \theta\in\Theta,\theta\neq\theta_{o}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The second component for consistency of the M-estimator is convergence of
 the sample average 
\begin_inset Formula $N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}q(w_{i},\theta)$
\end_inset

 to its expected value.
 It turns out that 
\series bold
point-wise convergence in probability
\series default
, as stated in equation (10), is not sufficient for consistency.
 That is, it is not enough to simply invoke the usual weak law of large
 numbets at each 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\in\Theta$
\end_inset

.
 Instead, 
\series bold
uniform convergence in probability
\series default
 is sufficient.
 Mathematically,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \max_{\theta\in\Theta}}\left|N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}q(w_{i},\theta)-E\left[q(w,\theta)\right]\right|\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Uniform convergence clearly implies pointwise convergence, but the converse
 is not true.
 Nevertheless, under certain regularity conditions, the pointwise convergence
 in equation (10) translates into the uniform convergence in equation (12).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since we are taking expected values of 
\begin_inset Formula $q(w,\theta)$
\end_inset

 with respect to the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $q(w,\theta)$
\end_inset

 must be a random variable for each 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\in\Theta$
\end_inset

.
 Technically, we should assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $q(\cdot,\theta)$
\end_inset

 is a 
\emph on
Borel measurable function
\emph default
 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{W}$
\end_inset

 for each 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\in\Theta$
\end_inset

.
 The next assumption concering 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 is practically more important.
 We assume that, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathscr{W}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $q(w,\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is a 
\emph on
continuous function
\emph default
 over the parameter space 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

.
 All of the problems we treat in detail have objective functions that are
 continuous in the parameters, but these do not cover all cases of interest.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Obtaining uniform convergence is generally difficult for unbouded parameter
 sets, such as 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta=\mathbb{R}^{P}$
\end_inset

.
 It is easiest to assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

 is a 
\emph on
compact subset
\emph default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{P}$
\end_inset

.
 Because the natural parameter spaces in most applications are not bounded
 (and sometimes not closed), the compactness assumption is untidy for developing
 a general theory of estimation.
 However, for must applications it is not an assumption to worry about:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

 can be defined to be such a large closed and bounded set as to always contain
 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can now state a theorem concerning uniform convergence appropriate for
 the random sampling environment.
 This result is known as the 
\series bold
uniform weak law of large numbers (UWLLN)
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
12.1 (Uniform Weak Law of Large Numbers).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 be a random vector taking values in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{W}\subset\mathbb{R}^{M}$
\end_inset

, let 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

 be subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{P}$
\end_inset

, and let 
\begin_inset Formula $q:\mathscr{W}\times\Theta\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 be a real-valued function.
 Assume that 
\series bold
(a)
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

 is compact; 
\series bold
(b)
\series default
 for each 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\in\Theta$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $q(\cdot,\theta)$
\end_inset

 is Borel measurable on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{W}$
\end_inset

; 
\series bold
(c)
\series default
 for each 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathscr{W}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $q(w,\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is continuous on 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

; and 
\series bold
(d)
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|q(w,\theta)\right|\leq b(w)$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\in\Theta$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 is a nonegative function on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{W}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[b(w)\right]<\infty$
\end_inset

.
 Then equation (12) holds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assumption d requires the expected absolute value of 
\begin_inset Formula $q(w,\theta)$
\end_inset

 to be bounded across 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

, but it is rarely verified in practice even though it can be.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The continuity and compactness assumptions are important for establishing
 uniform convergence, and they also ensure that both the sample minimization
 problem (8) and the population minimization problem (9) actually have solutions.
 Consider problem (8) first.
 Under the assumptions of Theorem 12.1, the sample average is a continuous
 function of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

, since 
\begin_inset Formula $q(w_{i},\theta)$
\end_inset

 is continuous for each 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Since a continuous function on a compact space always achieves its minimum,
 the M-estimation problem is well defined (there could be more than one
 solution).
 As a technical matter, it can be shown that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 is actually a random variable under the measurability assumption on 
\begin_inset Formula $q(\cdot,\theta)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It can also be shown that, under the assumptions of Theorem 12.1, the function
 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[q(w,\theta)\right]$
\end_inset

 is continuous as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, problem (9) also has at least one solution; identifiability
 ensures that it has only one olution, wich in turn implies consistency
 of the M-estimator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
12.2 (Consistency of M-Estimators).

\series default
 
\emph on
Under assumptions of Theorem 12.1, assume that the identification assumption
 (11) holds.
 Then a random vector, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

, solves problem (8), and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For nonlinear least squares, once Assumptions NLS.1 and NLS.2 are maintained,
 the practical requirement is that 
\begin_inset Formula $m(x,\cdot)$
\end_inset

 be a continuous funciton over 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

.
 This assumption is almost always true in application of NLS.
 Noncompactness of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

 is not much of a concern for most applications.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Lemma*

\series bold
\shape up
12.1.

\series default
 
\emph on
Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

, and assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $r(w,\theta)$
\end_inset

 satisfies the same assumptions on 
\begin_inset Formula $q(w,\theta)$
\end_inset

 in Theorem 12.2.
 Then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}r(w_{i},\hat{\theta})\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}E\left[r(w,\theta_{o})\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

That is, 
\begin_inset Formula $N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}r(w_{i},\hat{\theta})$
\end_inset

 is a consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[r(w,\theta_{o})\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Lemma 12.1 shows that, if we replace 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 in the sample average with a consistent estimator, the convergence still
 holds, at least under standard regularity conditions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
12.3 Asymptotic Normality
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The simplest asymptotic normality proof proceeds as follows.
 Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 is in the interior of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

, which means that 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

 must have nonempty interior; this assumption is true in most applications.
 Then, since 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 is in the interior of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

 with probability approaching one.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $q(w,\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is continuously differentiable on the interior of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

, then (with probablity approaching one) 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 solves the first-order condition
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s(w_{i},\hat{\theta})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $s(w,\theta)$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $P\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of partial derivatives of 
\begin_inset Formula $q(w,\theta):s(w,\theta)=\nabla_{\theta}q(w,\theta)\equiv\left[\nicefrac{\partial q(w,\theta)}{\partial\theta_{1}},\ldots,\nicefrac{\partial q(w,\theta)}{\partial\theta_{P}}\right]^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 We call 
\begin_inset Formula $s(w,\theta)$
\end_inset

 the 
\series bold
score of the objective function
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $q(w,\theta)$
\end_inset

.
 While condition (14) can only be guaranteed to hold with probability approachin
g one, usually it holds exactly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $q(w,\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is twice continuously differentiable, then each row of the left-hand side
 of equation (14) can be expanded about 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 in a mean-value expansion:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s(w_{i},\hat{\theta})={\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s(w_{i},\theta_{o})+\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\ddot{H}_{i}\right)(\hat{\theta}-\theta_{o})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The notation 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{H}_{i}$
\end_inset

 denotes the 
\begin_inset Formula $P\times P$
\end_inset

 
\series bold
Hessian of the object function
\series default
, 
\begin_inset Formula $q(w_{i},\theta)$
\end_inset

, with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

, but with each row of 
\begin_inset Formula $H(w_{i},\theta)\equiv\nicefrac{\partial^{2}q(w_{i},\theta)}{\partial\theta\partial\theta^{\prime}}\equiv\nabla_{\theta}^{2}q(w_{i},\theta)$
\end_inset

 evaluated at a different mean value.
 Each of the 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 mean values is on the line segment between 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

.
 We cannot know what these mean values are, but we do know that each must
 converge in probability to 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Combining equations (14) and (15) and multiplying through by 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{1}{\sqrt{N}}$
\end_inset

 gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
0=N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s(w_{i},\theta_{o})+\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\ddot{H}_{i}\right)\sqrt{N}(\hat{\theta}-\theta_{o})
\]

\end_inset

Now, we can apply Lemma 12.1 to get 
\begin_inset Formula $N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ddot{H}_{i}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}E\left[H(w,\theta_{o})\right]$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{o}\equiv E\left[H(w,\theta_{o})\right]$
\end_inset

 is nonsingular, then 
\begin_inset Formula $N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ddot{H}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is nonsingular w.p.a.1 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ddot{H}_{i}\right)^{-1}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}A_{o}^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, we can write
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sqrt{N}(\hat{\theta}-\theta_{o})=\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\ddot{H}_{i}\right)^{-1}\left[-N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}(\theta_{o})\right]
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}\equiv s(w_{i},\theta_{o})$
\end_inset

.
 As we will show, 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[s_{i}(\theta_{o})\right]=0$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}\sum_{i=1}^{N}s_{i}(\theta_{o})$
\end_inset

 generally satisfies the CLT because it is the average of i.i.d.
 random vectors with zero mean, multiplied by the usual 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $o_{p}(1)\cdot O_{p}(1)=o_{p}(1)$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sqrt{N}\left(\hat{\theta}-\theta_{o}\right)=A_{o}^{-1}\left[-N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}(\theta_{o})\right]+o_{p}(1)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This equation shows that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}\left(\hat{\theta}-\theta_{o}\right)$
\end_inset

 inherits its limiting distribution from the average of the scores, evaluated
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

.
 The matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{o}^{-1}$
\end_inset

 simply acts as a linear transformation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Equation (16) allows us to derive the 
\series bold
first-order asymptotic distribution
\series default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

.
 We have essentially proved the following result.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
12.3 (Asymptotic Normality of M-Estimators):
\series default
 
\emph on
In addition to the assumptions in Theorem 12.2, assume 
\series bold
(a)
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 is in the interior of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

; 
\series bold
(b)
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $s(w,\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is continuously differentiable on the interior of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathscr{W}$
\end_inset

; 
\series bold
(c)
\series default
 Each element of 
\begin_inset Formula $H(w,\theta)$
\end_inset

 is bounded in absolute value by a function 
\begin_inset Formula $b(w)$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[b(w)\right]<\infty$
\end_inset

; 
\series bold
(d)
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{o}\equiv E\left[H(w,\theta_{o})\right]$
\end_inset

 is positive definite; 
\series bold
(e)
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[s(w,\theta_{o})\right]=0$
\end_inset

; and 
\series bold
(f)
\series default
 each element of 
\begin_inset Formula $s(w,\theta_{o})$
\end_inset

 has finite second moment.
 Then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sqrt{N}\left(\hat{\theta}-\theta_{o}\right)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}N(0,A_{o}^{-1}B_{o}A_{o}^{-1})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
A_{o}\equiv E\left[H(w,\theta_{o})\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
B_{o}\equiv E\left[s(w,\theta_{o})s(w,\theta_{o})^{\prime}\right]=\mbox{Var}\left[s(w,\theta_{o})\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\theta})=\frac{A_{o}^{-1}B_{o}A_{o}^{-1}}{N}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Theorem 12.3 implies asymptotic normality of most of the remaining estimators
 we study.
 A key component of this theorem is that the score evaluated at 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 has expected value zero.
 If we can interchange the expectation and the derivative, then this result
 holds directly in the abstract M-estimation framework.
 To see this point, note that if 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 is in the interior of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[q(w,\theta)\right]$
\end_inset

 is differentiable for 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\in\mbox{int}\ \Theta$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\nabla_{\theta}E\left[q(w,\theta)\right]_{\theta=\theta_{o}}=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Now, if the derivative and expectations operator can be interchanged, wich
 is the case quite generally, then equation (21) implies
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E\left[\nabla_{\theta}q(w,\theta_{o})\right]=E\left[s(w,\theta_{o})\right]=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

A similar argument shows that, in general, 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[H(w,\theta_{o})\right]$
\end_inset

 is positive semidefinite.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 is identified, 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[H(w,\theta_{o})\right]$
\end_inset

 is positive definite.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For the remainder of this chapter, it is convneient to divide the original
 NLS objective function by two:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
q(w_{i},\theta)=\frac{\left[y_{i}-m(x_{i},\theta)\right]^{2}}{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The score of equation (23) can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
s(w_{i},\theta)=-\nabla_{\theta}m(x_{i},\theta)\left[y-m(x_{i},\theta)\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla_{\theta}m(x_{i},\theta)$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $P\times1$
\end_inset

 gradient of 
\begin_inset Formula $m(x,\theta)$
\end_inset

.
 We can show directly that this expression has an expected value of zero
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 by showing that epected value of 
\begin_inset Formula $s(w,\theta_{o})$
\end_inset

 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is zero:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E\left[s(w,\theta_{o})|x\right]=-\nabla_{\theta}m(x_{i},\theta)\left[E(y|x)-m(x_{i},\theta)\right]=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $s(w_{i},\theta_{o})$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
B_{o}\equiv E\left[s(w,\theta_{o})s(w,\theta_{o})^{\prime}\right]=E\left[u_{i}^{2}\nabla_{\theta}m(x_{i},\theta_{o})\nabla_{\theta}m(x_{i},\theta_{o})^{\prime}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the error 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}\equiv y-m(x_{i},\theta_{o})$
\end_inset

 is the difference between 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y|x)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Hessian of 
\begin_inset Formula $q(w,\theta)$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
H(w,\theta)=\nabla_{\theta}m(x_{i},\theta_{o})\nabla_{\theta}m(x_{i},\theta_{o})^{\prime}-\nabla_{\theta}^{2}m(x_{i},\theta)\left[y_{i}-m(x_{i},\theta)\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla_{\theta}^{2}m(x_{i},\theta)$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $P\times P$
\end_inset

 Hessian matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $m(x_{i},\theta)$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
 To find the expected value of 
\begin_inset Formula $H(w,\theta)$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

, we first find the expectation conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 When evaluated at 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

, the second term in equation (27) is 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla_{\theta}^{2}m(x_{i},\theta)u_{i}$
\end_inset

, and it therefore has a zero mean conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 since 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u|x)=0$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E\left[H(w,\theta_{o})|x\right]=\nabla_{\theta}m(x_{i},\theta_{o})\nabla_{\theta}m(x_{i},\theta_{o})^{\prime}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Taking the expected value of equation (28) over the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
A_{o}=E\left[\nabla_{\theta}m(x_{i},\theta_{o})\nabla_{\theta}m(x_{i},\theta_{o})^{\prime}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This matrix plays a fundamental role in nonlinear regression.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 is identified, 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{o}$
\end_inset

 is generally positive definite.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
12.5 Estimating the Asymptotic Variance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We wish to consistently estimate the matrices 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{o}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{o}$
\end_inset

.
 We can always estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{o}$
\end_inset

 consistently by taking away the expectaiton and replacing 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

.
 Under regularity conditions that ensure uniform convergence of the Hessian,
 the estimator
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}H(w_{i},\hat{\theta})\equiv N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{H}_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is consistent for 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{o}$
\end_inset

, by Lemma 12.1.
 Estimator (30) is always available in problems with a twice continuously
 differentiable objective function.
 This includes cases where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 does not index a feature of an unconditional or conditional distribution
 (such as nonlinear regression with the conditional mean function misspecified).
 Its main drawback is computational: it requires calculation of second derivativ
es, which is a nontrivial task for certian problems.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Because we assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 solves the minimzation problem in equation (8), and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 is in the interior of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

, we know that the Hessian evaluated at 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 is at least p.s.d.
 If the model is well specified in the sense that 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 is identified, then the estimator in (30) will actually be positive definite
 with high probability because 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[H(w_{i},\theta_{o})\right]$
\end_inset

 is positive definite.
 In some cases, the objective funciton is strictly convex on 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

, in which case 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i=1}^{N}H(w_{i},\theta)$
\end_inset

 is positive definite for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\in\Theta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Obtaining a p.s.d.
 estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{o}$
\end_inset

 is straightforward.
 By Lemma 12.1, under standard regularity conditions we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s(w_{i},\hat{\theta})s(w_{i},\hat{\theta})^{\prime}\equiv N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{s}_{i}\hat{s}_{i}^{\prime}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}B_{o}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Combining the estimator (31) with the consistent estimator for 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{o}$
\end_inset

, we can consistently estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Avar}\sqrt{N}(\hat{\theta}-\theta_{o})$
\end_inset

 by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\theta}-\theta_{o})}=\hat{A}^{-1}\hat{B}\hat{A}^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The asymptotic standard errors are obtained from the matrix
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{V}\equiv\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\theta})}=\frac{\hat{A}^{-1}\hat{B}\hat{A}^{-1}}{N}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which can be expressed as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{H}_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{s}_{i}\hat{s}_{i}^{\prime}}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{H}_{i}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Expression (34) is at least p.s.d.
 when it is well-defined.
 The variance matrix estimator in (34) is an example of a 
\series bold
Huber-White sandwhich estimator
\series default
, and it is a 
\series bold
fully robust variance matrix estimator
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the case of NLS, the estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{o}$
\end_inset

 in equation (30) is always available when 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})=m(x_{i},\theta_{o})$
\end_inset

, and it is usually used if we think the mean function is correctly specified:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\nabla_{\theta}\hat{m}_{i}\nabla_{\theta}\hat{m}_{i}^{\prime}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla_{\theta}\hat{m}_{i}\equiv\nabla_{\theta}m(x_{i},\hat{\theta})$
\end_inset

 for every observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 Also, the estimated score for NLS can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{s}_{i}=-\nabla_{\theta}\hat{m}_{i}\left[y_{i}-m(x_{i},\hat{\theta})\right]=-\nabla_{\theta}\hat{m}_{i}\hat{u}_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the 
\series bold
nonlinear least squares residuals
\series default
, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

, are defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{u}_{i}\equiv y_{i}-m(x_{i},\hat{\theta})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The estimated asymptotic variance of the NLS estimator is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\theta})}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\nabla_{\theta}\hat{m}_{i}\nabla_{\theta}\hat{m}_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{u}_{i}^{2}\nabla_{\theta}\hat{m}_{i}\nabla_{\theta}\hat{m}_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\nabla_{\theta}\hat{m}_{i}\nabla_{\theta}\hat{m}_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This is called the 
\series bold
heteroskedasticity-robust variance matrix estimator for NLS
\series default
 because it places no restrictions on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(y|x)$
\end_inset

.
 As always, the asymptotic standard error of each element of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 is the square root of the appropriate diagonal element of matrix (38).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In many contexts, including NLS, the asymptotic variance estimator can be
 simplified under additional assumptions.
 For our purposes, we state the assumption as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E\left[s(w_{i},\theta_{o})s(w_{i},\theta_{o})^{\prime}\right]\equiv\sigma_{o}^{2}E\left[H(w_{i},\theta_{o})\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This assumption simply says that the expected outer product of the score,
 evaluated at 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

, is proportional to the expected value of the Hessian (evaluated at 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

): 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{o}=\sigma_{o}^{2}A_{o}$
\end_inset

.
 We refer to assumption (35) as the 
\series bold
generalized information matrix equality (GIME)
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Lemma*

\series bold
\shape up
12.2:
\series default
 
\emph on
Under regularity conditions of the type contained in Theorem 12.3 and assumption
 (35), 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\theta})=\nicefrac{\sigma_{o}^{2}A_{o}^{-1}}{N}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, under assumption (35), the asymptotic variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 can be estimated as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{V}=\hat{\sigma}^{2}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{H}_{i}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is defined as before, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}\sigma_{o}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the case of nonlinear regression, the parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{o}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, or equivalently 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(u|x)$
\end_inset

, under homoskedasiticty:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\shape up
NLS.3:
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(y|x)=\mbox{Var}(u|x)=\sigma_{o}^{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Under Assumption NLS.3, we can show that assumption (39) holds with 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{o}^{2}=\mbox{Var}(y|x)$
\end_inset

.
 First, since 
\begin_inset Formula $s(w_{i},\theta_{o})s(w_{i},\theta_{o})^{\prime}=u_{i}^{2}\nabla_{\theta}m(x_{i},\theta_{o})\nabla_{\theta}m(x_{i},\theta_{o})^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, it follows that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E\left[s(w_{i},\theta_{o})s(w_{i},\theta_{o})^{\prime}|x\right] & = & E(u_{i}^{2}|x)\nabla_{\theta}m(x_{i},\theta_{o})\nabla_{\theta}m(x_{i},\theta_{o})^{\prime}\nonumber \\
 & = & \sigma_{o}^{2}\nabla_{\theta}m(x_{i},\theta_{o})\nabla_{\theta}m(x_{i},\theta_{o})^{\prime}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

under Assumptions NLS.1 and NLS.3.
 Taking the expected value with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 gives equation (39).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Under Assumption NLS.3, a simplified estimator of the asymptotic variance
 of the NLS estiamtor exists from equation (41).
 Let
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\sigma}^{2}=\frac{1}{N-P}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{u}_{i}^{2}=\frac{SSR}{N-P}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

 are the NLS residuals and SSR is the sum of squared NLS residuals.
 Using Lemmas 12.1, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}$
\end_inset

 can be shown to be consistent very generally.
 The subtraction of 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 in the denominator of equation (38) is an adjustment that is thought to
 improve the small sample properties of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Under Assumptions NLS.1-NLS.3, the asymptotic variance of the NLS esitmator
 is estimated as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\sigma}^{2}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\nabla_{\theta}\hat{m}_{i}\nabla_{\theta}\hat{m}_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This is the default variance estimator for NLS, but it is valid only under
 homoskedasticity; the estimator (38) is valid with or without Assumption
 NLS.3.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
12.6 Hypothesis Testing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We wish to test the 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

 restrictions 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:c(\theta_{o})=0$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $c:\mathbb{R}^{P}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{Q}$
\end_inset

.
 In these notes, when taking the derivative of a scalar function with respect
 to a column vector, we obtain the gradient, which we treat as a column
 vector.
 As a result, let 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\nabla_{\theta}c(\theta_{o})\equiv\frac{\partial\left[c(\theta_{o})\right]^{\prime}}{\partial\theta}$
\end_inset

, which is a 
\begin_inset Formula $P\times Q$
\end_inset

 matrix.
 Thus, to simply notation and keep it consistent with the book, define 
\begin_inset Formula $C(\theta_{o})$
\end_inset

 to be the transpose of 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla_{\theta}c(\theta_{o})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
12.6.1 Wald Tests
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Wald tests are easily obtained.
 To test the 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

 restrictions 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:c(\theta_{o})=0$
\end_inset

, we can form the Wald statistic
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
W\equiv c(\hat{\theta})^{\prime}(\hat{C}\hat{V}\hat{C}^{\prime})^{-1}c(\hat{\theta})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{V}$
\end_inset

is an asymptotic variance matrix estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{C}\equiv C(\hat{\theta})$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $C(\theta)$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $Q\times P$
\end_inset

 Jacobian of 
\begin_inset Formula $c(\theta)$
\end_inset

.
 The estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{V}$
\end_inset

 can be chosen to be fully robust, as in expression (34); under assumption
 (39), the simpler form in Lemma 12.2 is available.
 Provided 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{V}$
\end_inset

 has been chosen appropriately, 
\begin_inset Formula $W\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{a}\\
\sim
\end{array}\chi_{Q}^{2}$
\end_inset

 under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A couple of practical restrictions are needed for 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 to have a limiting 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi_{Q}^{2}$
\end_inset

 distribution.
 First, 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 must be in ther interior of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

; that is, 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 cannot be on the boundary.
 If, for example, the first element of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 must be nonnegative, and we impose this restriction in the estimation,
 then expression (44) does not have a limiting chi-square distribution under
 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:\theta_{o1}=0$
\end_inset

.
 The second condition is that 
\begin_inset Formula $C(\theta_{o})=\left[\nabla_{\theta}c(\theta_{o})\right]^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 must have rank 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

.
 This rules out cases where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 is unidentified under the null hypothesis.
 One drawback to the Wald statistic is that it is not invariant to how the
 nonlinear restrictions are imposed.
 We can change the outcome of a hypothesis test by redefining the constraint
 function, 
\begin_inset Formula $c(\cdot)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
12.6.2 Score (or Lagrange Multiplier) Tests
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In cases where the unrestricted model is difficult to estimate but the restriced
 model is relatively simple to estimate, it is convenient to have a statistic
 that only requires estimation under the null.
 Such a statistic is the 
\series bold
score statistic
\series default
, also called the 
\series bold
Lagrange multiplier statistic
\series default
 in econometrics.
 Note that the score principle is applicable to any problem where the estimators
 solve a first-order condition, including the general class of M-estimators.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The score approach is ideally suited for 
\series bold
specification testing
\series default
.
 Typically, the first step in specification testing is to being with a popular
 model, one that is relatively easy to estimate and interpret, and nest
 it within a more complicated model.
 Then the popular model is tested against the more general alternative to
 determine if the original model is misspecified.
 We do not want to estimate the more complicated model unless there is significa
nt evidence against the restricted form of the model.
 In stating the null and alternative hypotheses, there is no difference
 between specification testing and classical tests of parameter restrictions.
 However, in practice, specification testing gives primary importance to
 the restricted model, and we have no intention of actually estimating the
 general model even if the null model is rejected.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To derive the score test, we again assume that there are 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

 continuously differentiable restrictions imposed on 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $c(\theta_{o})=0$
\end_inset

.
 However, we must also assume that the restrictions define a mapping from
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{P-Q}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{P}$
\end_inset

, say 
\begin_inset Formula $d:\mathbb{R}^{P-Q}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{P}$
\end_inset

.
 In particular, under the null hypothesis, we can write 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}=d(\lambda_{o})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{o}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $(P-Q)\times1$
\end_inset

 vector.
 We must assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{o}$
\end_inset

 is in the interior of its parameter space, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda$
\end_inset

, under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 We also assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

 is twice continuously differentiable on the interior of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\lambda}$
\end_inset

be the solution to the constrained minimization problem
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \min_{\lambda\in\Lambda}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}q\left[w_{i},d(\lambda)\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The constrined estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 is simply 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\theta}\equiv d(\tilde{\lambda})$
\end_inset

.
 In practice, we do not have to explicitly find the funciton 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

; solving problem (45) is easily done just by directly imposing the restrictions
, especially when the restrictions set certain parameters to hypothesized
 values (such as zero).
 Then, we just minimize the resulting objective function over the free parameter
s.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The LM statistic is based on the limiting distribution of
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}(\tilde{\theta})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 This is the score with respect to the entire vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

, but we are evaluating it at the restricted estimates.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\theta}$
\end_inset

 were replaced by 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

, then expression (46) would be identically zero, which would make it useless
 as a test statistic.
 If the restrictions imposed by the null hypothesis are true, then expression
 (46) will not be statistically different from zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Assume initially that 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 is in the interior of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

 under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 Now, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}(\tilde{\theta}-\theta_{o})=O_{p}(1)$
\end_inset

 by the delta method because 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}(\tilde{\lambda}-\lambda_{o})=O_{p}(1)$
\end_inset

 under the given assumptions.
 A standard mean value expansion yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}(\tilde{\theta})=N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}(\theta_{o})+A_{o}\sqrt{N}(\tilde{\theta}-\theta_{o})+o_{p}(1)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{o}$
\end_inset

 is given in expression (18).
 But 
\begin_inset Formula $0=\sqrt{N}c(\tilde{\theta})=\sqrt{N}c(\theta_{o})+\ddot{C}\sqrt{N}(\tilde{\theta}-\theta_{o})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{C}$
\end_inset

is the 
\begin_inset Formula $Q\times P$
\end_inset

 Jacobian matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $C(\theta)$
\end_inset

 with rows evaluated at mean values between 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\theta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

.
 Under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $c(\theta_{o})=0$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{p\lim}\ddot{C}=C(\theta_{o})\equiv C_{o}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{o}\sqrt{N}(\tilde{\theta}-\theta_{o})=o_{p}(1)$
\end_inset

, and so multiplying equation (47) through by 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{o}A_{o}^{-1}$
\end_inset

 gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
C_{o}A_{o}^{-1}N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}(\tilde{\theta})=C_{o}A_{o}^{-1}N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}(\theta_{o})+o_{p}(1)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

By the CLT, 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{o}A_{o}^{-1}N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}\sum_{i=1}^{N}s_{i}(\theta_{o})\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}N\left(0,C_{o}A_{o}^{-1}B_{o}A_{o}^{-1}C_{o}^{\prime}\right)$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{o}$
\end_inset

 is defined in expression (19).
 Under our assumptions, 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{o}A_{o}^{-1}B_{o}A_{o}^{-1}C_{o}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 has full rank 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

, and so
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}(\tilde{\theta})\right]^{\prime}A_{o}^{-1}C_{o}^{\prime}\left(C_{o}A_{o}^{-1}B_{o}A_{o}^{-1}C_{o}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}C_{o}A_{o}^{-1}\left[N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}(\tilde{\theta})\right]\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}\chi_{Q}^{2}
\]

\end_inset

The score or LM statistic is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
LM\equiv N^{-1}\cdot\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\tilde{s}_{i}\right)^{\prime}\tilde{A}^{-1}\tilde{C}^{\prime}\left(\tilde{C}\tilde{A}^{-1}\tilde{B}\tilde{A}^{-1}\tilde{C}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\tilde{C}\tilde{A}^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\tilde{s}_{i}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where all quantities are evaluated at 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\theta}$
\end_inset

.
 For example, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{C}\equiv C(\tilde{\theta})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{B}$
\end_inset

 is given in expression (31), but with 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\theta}$
\end_inset

 in place of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{A}$
\end_inset

 is the estimator in expression (30), again evaluated at 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\theta}$
\end_inset

.
 Under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $LM\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}\chi_{Q}^{2}$
\end_inset

 .
 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{B}$
\end_inset

 is at least p.s.d., 
\begin_inset Formula $LM\geq0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The general derivation of the LM statistic also assumed that 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}\in\mbox{int}(\Theta)$
\end_inset

 under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 Nevertheless, for certain applications of the LM test we can drop the requireme
nt that 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 is in the interior of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

 under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 A leading case occurs when 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 can be partitioned as 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\equiv(\theta_{1},\theta_{2})^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{1}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $(P-Q)\times1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{2}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $Q\times1$
\end_inset

.
 The null hypothesis is 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:\theta_{o2}=0$
\end_inset

, so that 
\begin_inset Formula $c(\theta)\equiv\theta_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 It is easy to see that the mean value expansion used to derive the LM statistic
 is valid provided 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{o}\equiv\theta_{o1}$
\end_inset

 is in the interior of its parameter space under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}\equiv(\theta_{o1},0)^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 can be on the boundary of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

.
 This observation is useful especially when testing hypotheses about parameters
 that must be either nonegative or nonpositive.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
12.6.3 Tests Based on the Change in the Objective Function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When both the restricted and unrestriced models are easy to estimate, a
 test based on the change in the objective function can greatly simplify
 the mechanics of obtaining a test statistic: we only need to obtain the
 value of the objective function with and without the restrictions imposed.
 However, the computational simplicity comes at a price in terms of robustness.
 Unlike the Wald and score tests, a test based on the change in the objective
 funciton 
\emph on
cannot
\emph default
 be made robust to general failure of assumption (39).
 Therefore, throughout this subsection, we assume that the generalized informati
on matrix equality holds.
 Because the minimized objective function is invariance with respect to
 reparmeterization, the test statistic is invariance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We first consider the case where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{o}^{2}=1$
\end_inset

, so that 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{o}=A_{o}$
\end_inset

.
 Using a second-order Taylor expansion,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}q(w_{i},\tilde{\theta})-{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}q(w_{i},\hat{\theta})=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}(\hat{\theta})\right)(\hat{\theta}-\theta_{o})+(\nicefrac{1}{2})(\tilde{\theta}-\hat{\theta})^{\prime}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\ddot{H}_{i}\right)(\tilde{\theta}-\hat{\theta})
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{H}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $P\times P$
\end_inset

 Hessian evaluated at mean values between 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\theta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

 (using the first-order condition for 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

), we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
2\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}q(w_{i},\tilde{\theta})-{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}q(w_{i},\hat{\theta})\right]=\left[\sqrt{N}(\tilde{\theta}-\hat{\theta})\right]^{\prime}A_{o}\left[\sqrt{N}(\tilde{\theta}-\hat{\theta})\right]+o_{p}(1)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

since 
\begin_inset Formula $N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ddot{H}_{i}=A_{o}+o_{p}(1)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}(\tilde{\theta}-\hat{\theta})=O_{p}(1)$
\end_inset

.
 In fact, it follows from equation (47) that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}(\tilde{\theta}-\hat{\theta})=A_{o}^{-1}N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}\sum_{i=1}^{N}s_{i}(\tilde{\theta})+o_{p}(1)$
\end_inset

.
 Plugging this into equation (50) shows that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
QLR & \equiv & 2\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}q(w_{i},\tilde{\theta})-{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}q(w_{i},\hat{\theta})\right]\nonumber \\
 & = & \left(N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\tilde{s}_{i}\right)^{\prime}A_{o}^{-1}\left(N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\tilde{s}_{i}\right)+o_{p}(1)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

so that QLR has the same limiting distribution, 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi_{Q}^{2}$
\end_inset

, as the LM statistic under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 We call statistic (51) the 
\series bold
quasi-likelihood ratio (QLR) statistic
\series default
, which comes from the fact that the leading example of equation (51) is
 the likelihood ratio statistic in the contect of maximum likelihood estimation.
 We could call equation (51) a 
\series bold
criterion function statistic
\series default
, as it is based on the difference in the criterion or objective function
 with and without the restrictions imposed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{o}^{2}\neq1$
\end_inset

, we simply divide OLR by 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}$
\end_inset

, which is a consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{o}^{2}$
\end_inset

 obtained from the unrestricted estimation.
 For example, consider NLS under Assumptions NLS.1-NLS.3.
 When equation (51) is divided by 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}$
\end_inset

 in equation (42), we obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{(SSR_{r}-SSR_{ur})}{\left[\nicefrac{SSR_{ur}}{(N-P)}\right]}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $SSR_{r}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $SSR_{ur}$
\end_inset

 are the restricted and unrestricted sum of squared residuals.
 Sometimes an 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

version of this statistic is used instead, which is obtained by dividing
 the chi-square version by 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F=\frac{SSR_{r}-SSR_{ur}}{SSR_{ur}}\cdot\frac{(N-P)}{Q}
\]

\end_inset

This has exactly the same form as the 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 statistic from classical linear regression analysis.
 Under the null hypothesis and homoskedasticity, 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 can be treated as having an approximate 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{F}_{Q,N-P}$
\end_inset

 distribution.
\end_layout

